findings.evidence:
After orbAnimSync, each orb tick (DEFAULT_HZ 30 at :155, about every 2nd frame at 50 fps) still does the following.

Renderer main thread: rewrites 6 SVG path `d` attributes per vessel (createDriver tick, :961-966: back, body, clip, 3 meniscus) plus the .wsurf SVGTransform (:976), so every tick pays SVG path parse, layout, paint and commit ×3.

CrGpuMain, GPU raster of each 92-px vessel box (contain:paint) with these live effects:
- `.subglow { filter: blur(6px); mix-blend-mode: screen }` (:634) and `.sspec` blur(2.6px) + screen (:638-641), both riding the per-tick .wsurf translate;
- mana `.shimmer` blur(6px) (:662);
- `.caustics { mix-blend-mode: screen }` (:646), clipped to the live wave path, plus `.lqwall { mix-blend-mode: multiply }` (:637), also clipped to the live wave, which gives two anti-aliased wave-path clip masks per vessel per raster. Multiply is not a coefficient blend mode, so Skia needs a destination copy on ANGLE D3D11 (no KHR_blend_equation_advanced);
- 3 animated bubbles per vessel (:655).

The file's own perf notes record that these layers dominate: v1 cost 41% of the render pipeline, and live caustics were 'HALF the whole HUD's render pipeline' before baking. The 10-06 trace put DOM raster at 4.7 ms/frame on CrGpuMain (fps-review finding 1), when the loops still ran at display rate. The post-trim share is unmeasured (open lead).

findings.proposal:
Behind `?orbGl=on` (default off until eye-tested): replace each pane's `<svg class="orb">` with a `<canvas>` of the same CSS box, transferred to one worker via transferControlToOffscreen. Alternatively, a single worker WebGL2 context rendering 3 ImageBitmaps into bitmaprenderer canvases.

Per cadence tick, the main thread posts only {level, phase, amp, impulse, vitae, low, keyframe samples} for the 3 orbs. The JS math already exists: createDriver.wave() and the hbo-* keyframes evaluated at the orbAnimSync phase.

One full-quad fragment shader per vessel computes:
- the two-sine wave line analytically (same formula, same STEP sampling optional) and the back wave at 0.85 opacity;
- the objectBoundingBox vertical absorption gradient;
- the wall darkening as exact multiply math (no destination copy);
- the baked CAUSTIC and GLASS PNGs (already produced by bakeRaster) as textures, masked by the wave;
- subglow/sspec/shimmer as analytic Gaussian ellipses with the same transform and opacity curves, and bubbles as analytic discs;
- the meniscus as distance-to-curve bands;
- vitae and fracture states as uniforms or textures.

Hit gate, plaque, heraldry and window positioning stay DOM, so nothing else changes. The main three.js renderer is untouched, which avoids any composer or program-key risk.

